<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Performance on harder math benchmarks improves significantly with GSM8K RL curriculum training stages. Bootstrapping simple existing data can be used for scaling RL. AIME avg@32.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This transfer patterns aligns with our <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pass@k</span> capability improvement results from Section <a href="#S4" title="4 In-domain results and the importance of curriculum ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and our theoretical framing. Tasks requiring sequential dependent reasoning, such as AIME or GSM-Symbolic problems, benefit from improved long-horizon reasoning capabilities that were learned on much simpler composed tasks. Crucially, improvements in aspects such as state-tracking (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) are also observable from our long-context evals. Our results indicate that a curriculum of simple explicit-horizon tasks can bootstrap advanced reasoning, providing a scalable path where composing problems at the edge of what can be solved would push capabilities further without new annotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, under our long-horizon skill model in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, attempting to train directly on long-horizon data with outcome rewards results in vanishing gradient signal, as very few rollouts achieve any reward. Curriculum training overcomes this by initially training at short horizons, where this signal is stronger. Raising the success rate in achieving a reward at horizon <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> also raises the success rate for horizons <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, and so when we come to train at <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the signal is no longer vanishing. In the analysis below, we prove that this is the case in our simplified long-horizon skills model, and demonstrate an exponential decrease (in the horizon length <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>) in the sample complexity for curriculum training vs direct outcome reward-only horizon <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> training, along with an equivalence between curriculum training and training at horizon <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with dense, per-step rewards.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study a simplified model of long-horizon correctness over <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> steps. Let <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate correctness at step <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{&lt;j}:=\prod_{\ell&lt;j}C_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{&lt;j}:=\prod_{\ell&lt;j}C_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we factor stepwise correctness as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}\;:=\;\Pr(C_{j}{=}1\mid C_{&lt;j}{=}1)\;=\;p(\theta_{0})\,\sigma_{j}(\theta_{j})\in(0,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}\;:=\;\Pr(C_{j}{=}1\mid C_{&lt;j}{=}1)\;=\;p(\theta_{0})\,\sigma_{j}(\theta_{j})\in(0,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\theta_{0})\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\theta_{0})\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">atomic reliability</em> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}(\theta_{j})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}(\theta_{j})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">horizon–dependent reliability</em> with <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Parameters are separated by depth: a shared atomic block <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and disjoint depth blocks <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This models our assumption that training strictly below depth <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> cannot alter <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (but does alter <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>). The probability the first <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> steps are all correct is <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}:=\prod_{j=1}^{i}q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}:=\prod_{j=1}^{i}q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rewards, objective, and estimator.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a chosen training horizon <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{1,\dots,H\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{1,\dots,H\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the verifier pays terminal reward <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{h}(y)=\mathbb{I}\{C_{\leq h}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{h}(y)=\mathbb{I}\{C_{\leq h}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the objective is <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[R_{h}]=s_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}[R_{h}]=s_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use REINFORCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams, 1992</a>)</cite> with an unbiased advantage estimator with <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constant</em> baseline equal to the same-horizon mean <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{h}=s_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{h}=s_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (the form approximated by GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>):</p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\big[(R_{h}-\mu_{h})\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y)\big],\qquad\bar{g}_{k}\;=\;\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(R_{h}(y_{i})-\mu_{h})\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}\big[(R_{h}-\mu_{h})\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y)\big],\qquad\bar{g}_{k}\;=\;\tfrac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(R_{h}(y_{i})-\mu_{h})\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant, <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\bar{g}_{k}]=\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\bar{g}_{k}]=\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expected improvement</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a></cite> in analyzing the expected improvement in our policy <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> when taking the gradient update step <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}^{+}=\theta_{k}+\eta\bar{g}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}^{+}=\theta_{k}+\eta\bar{g}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (holding other parameters fixed), the standard SGD argument (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duchi, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bubeck, 2015</a></cite>) yields</p>
<table id="S6.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[J_{h}(\theta^{+})-J_{h}(\theta)\right]\;\geq\;\underbrace{\frac{\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}(\theta)\|^{2}}{2L}}_{:=\,\Delta^{(0)}_{k,h}}\cdot\frac{1}{1+1/\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})}\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[J_{h}(\theta^{+})-J_{h}(\theta)\right]\;\geq\;\underbrace{\frac{\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}J_{h}(\theta)\|^{2}}{2L}}_{:=\,\Delta^{(0)}_{k,h}}\cdot\frac{1}{1+1/\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k}):=\|\mathbb{E}\bar{g}_{k}\|^{2}/\mathbb{E}\|\bar{g}_{k}-\mathbb{E}\bar{g}_{k}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>𝔼</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k}):=\|\mathbb{E}\bar{g}_{k}\|^{2}/\mathbb{E}\|\bar{g}_{k}-\mathbb{E}\bar{g}_{k}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For completeness, we give a proof in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Theoretical Analysis ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. To attain a fraction <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of the <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">noiseless gain</em> <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{(0)}_{k,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{(0)}_{k,h}</annotation></semantics></math> it suffices that</p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})\;\geq\;\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})\;\geq\;\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, SNR directly governs the batch size needed for a meaningful step. For terminal-only rewards and our parameter separation, the scaling of the SNR with batch size and success probability (proved in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Signal-to-noise ratio ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Analysis ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>) is</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})\;=\;\Theta\!\big(N\,s_{h}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}(\theta_{k})\;=\;\Theta\!\big(N\,s_{h}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">because <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability mass on informative trajectories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Direct full-horizon training (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=H</annotation></semantics></math>).</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When trained only at the full horizon, the end-to-end success <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is exponentially small in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> at initialization (under <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}\in[\delta,1-\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}\in[\delta,1-\delta]</annotation></semantics></math>), so the estimator’s signal-to-noise ratio scales like <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}=\Theta(N\,\alpha^{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SNR</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}=\Theta(N\,\alpha^{H})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. To achieve a constant fraction of the noiseless improvement one therefore needs a batch size exponential in the horizon length <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, while the noiseless gain itself decays exponentially (see proofs in App. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Regimes and consequences ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Analysis ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S6.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta(\alpha^{-H}),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,H}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\alpha^{2H}\,\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta(\alpha^{-H}),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,H}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\alpha^{2H}\,\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Curriculum over depths.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a target end-to-end success level <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> that we want the final policy to achieve at horizon <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. a deployment or evaluation threshold). A <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">curriculum</em> is a schedule that trains at increasing depths <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1,2,\ldots,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1,2,\ldots,H</annotation></semantics></math>, advancing from depth <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> only when the depth-<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> step is mastered. Concretely, we use the promotion rule <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}\geq 1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}\geq 1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen so that <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\varepsilon)^{H}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\varepsilon)^{H}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>; equivalently <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim(-\ln c)/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim(-\ln c)/H</annotation></semantics></math>. This guarantees that if each depth meets the promotion criterion, then <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{H}=\prod_{j=1}^{H}q_{j}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{H}=\prod_{j=1}^{H}q_{j}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> at the end of the curriculum. During training at depth <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> we assume earlier depths have already been learned (and that forgetting is unlikely to occur as each prior task is a subtask of the latter ones), giving a depth-independent lower bound <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k-1}\in[c,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k-1}\in[c,1]</annotation></semantics></math> on the probability of reaching step <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Near the promotion point <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}=1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}=1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> the estimator’s SNR scales as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{k}(\theta_{k})\asymp N\,\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{k}(\theta_{k})\asymp N\,\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>; thus maintaining a constant fraction of the noiseless improvement requires only a batch linear in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, while the noiseless gain is horizon-independent (details in App. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Regimes and consequences ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Analysis ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S6.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta\!\left(\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right)\;=\;\Theta(H),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,k}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ε</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta\!\left(\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right)\;=\;\Theta(H),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,k}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Provided <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> does not decay faster than polynomially as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}\to 1-\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}\to 1-\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, curriculum training yields overall polynomial sample complexity in <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Relation to dense rewards.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Replace the terminal signal with per-step rewards <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{\prime}_{t}=\mathbb{I}\{C_{\leq t}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}_{t}=\mathbb{I}\{C_{\leq t}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and use the reward-to-go estimator at depth <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{k}\;=\;\big(G_{k}-b_{k}\big)\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y),\qquad G_{k}\;:=\;\sum_{t=k}^{h}R^{\prime}_{t},\qquad b_{k}\;:=\;\mathbb{E}[G_{k}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.391em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{k}\;=\;\big(G_{k}-b_{k}\big)\,\nabla_{\theta_{k}}\log\pi_{\theta}(y),\qquad G_{k}\;:=\;\sum_{t=k}^{h}R^{\prime}_{t},\qquad b_{k}\;:=\;\mathbb{E}[G_{k}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The dense reward SNR (<math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{dense}}(\theta_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>dense</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{dense}}(\theta_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>) is given in App. <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Dense rewards ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Analysis ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>, and depends on the ratio between the variance and mean squared of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under our assumption that <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}\in[\delta,1-\delta]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}\in[\delta,1-\delta]</annotation></semantics></math> at initialization, this ratio grows as <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(1)</annotation></semantics></math> with constants depending only on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the SNR scaling is controlled by the success probability <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to the SNR being large for short horizons, and small for long horizons at initialization. We therefore see a gradually increasing <em id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frontier</em> horizon <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the point at which <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k-1}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k-1}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> (for fixed <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>). At this frontier <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{dense}}(\theta_{k})=\Theta(N\,\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>dense</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{dense}}(\theta_{k})=\Theta(N\,\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, the same as curriculum training at horizon <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, and we see the same scaling in the required batch size and noiseless improvement:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta\!\left(\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right)\;=\;\Theta(H),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,\mathrm{dense}}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ε</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>dense</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N\;=\;\Theta\!\left(\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right)\;=\;\Theta(H),\qquad\Delta^{(0)}_{k,\mathrm{dense}}\;=\;\Theta\!\big(\|\nabla_{\theta_{k}}q_{k}\|^{2}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This improvement in sample complexity with dense rewards corresponds with results from similar work on reward shaping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laud, 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laud &amp; DeJong, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="119.27" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 119.27" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,119.27) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 101.23 C 0 104.49 2.64 107.13 5.91 107.13 L 544.09 107.13 C 547.36 107.13 550 104.49 550 101.23 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F0F0FF;" fill="#F0F0FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 101.23 C 1.97 103.4 3.73 105.16 5.91 105.16 L 544.09 105.16 C 546.27 105.16 548.03 103.4 548.03 101.23 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15 97.13)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_nestedsvg" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 91.08 22.14 C 92.72 22.14 94.04 20.82 94.04 19.19 L 94.04 2.95 C 94.04 1.32 92.72 0 91.08 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 2.95 L 0 19.19 C 0 20.82 1.32 22.14 2.95 22.14 L 91.08 22.14 C 92.72 22.14 94.04 20.82 94.04 19.19 L 94.04 2.95 C 94.04 1.32 92.72 0 91.08 0 L 2.95 0 C 1.32 0 0 1.32 0 2.95 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 11.81 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="70.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Key Insight</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 14.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:5.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="78.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 76.03)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">With terminal-only rewards, the signal-to-noise ratio scales with the success rate, making direct full-horizon training exponentially inefficient in the horizon length. Curriculum learning or dense rewards solve this by progressively raising the success rate at increasing horizons, reducing the sample complexity required to make meaningful policy improvements from exponential to polynomial.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Designing a cost-efficient curriculum</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In most real-world scenarios, there is an abundance of short-horizon data, and long-horizon data is expensive to obtain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwa et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sinha et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In this section, we ask whether long-horizon performance can be obtained from training data distributions that are “cheaper” than a uniform one. Namely, whether we can train on more short data and less long data and still achieve the same performance. We also evaluate how much this changes the training compute required.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07312v1/dctradeoff_updated_done.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/174;" width="548" height="174"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S7.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Sample count distributions for four settings. <span id="S7.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Middle</span>: Comparing accuracy at each stage across sample count settings. Under mild skew towards shorter samples like Setting 1 and 2, the model can perform as well as the uniform sample baseline. <span id="S7.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Comparing the training compute across settings. The settings skewed towards shorter samples have more training cost in terms of training tokens seen. Overall, low-cost data distributions can achieve near-optimal performance.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments follow the same curriculum RL method described in Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4 In-domain results and the importance of curriculum ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. During training, we train up to saturation for each stage, spending as much training compute as needed until there are no further improvements in accuracy. We track the total number of tokens seen by the model during training. We create four different curricula with the same total number of samples, and different proportions of short- and long-horizon data (Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7 Designing a cost-efficient curriculum ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Left).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Figure <a href="#S7.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 7 Designing a cost-efficient curriculum ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (Middle, Right) show that high long-horizon performance can be achieved even in data-constrained scenarios with training data distributions skewed towards shorter examples, but the trade-off is that more training compute needs to be spent overall. Understanding this trade-off is crucial for creating cost-effective datasets geared towards long-horizon reasoning tasks. Therefore, to further study this, we simplify our experimental setup to a SFT setting on a simpler task (multiplication), and scale up the search space for comprehensive evaluations. In Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Decreasing Sample Complexity for Longer Training Data ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> we provide results that detailed show, for a target accuracy, a similar trade-off exists between (1) training cost and (2) training compute budget. As long as more training compute is spent (discussed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Decreasing Sample Complexity for Longer Training Data ‣ h1: Bootstrapping LLMs to Reason over Longer Horizons via Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>), high performance can be achieved under settings with limited long-horizon data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce a new method for improving long-horizon reasoning in large language models. Our method leverages existing short-horizon data by composing new, multi-step problems through a chaining process. This approach allows us to scale reinforcement learning training via a curriculum, yielding substantial performance gains on multi-step reasoning tasks. An important result of our work is that the skills learned through this curriculum transfer to new, challenging reasoning and long-context tasks. Furthermore, our results show that the trained model learns genuinely new reasoning capabilities, rather than just refining existing ones. We also demonstrate that comparable performance can be achieved even when there is abundant short-horizon data but limited long-horizon data, thus providing a scalable and data-efficient path for improving frontier models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the goal of our paper is to introduce an early method for improving long-horizon reasoning, we see two promising directions for extensions. One is incorporating new sources of atomic skills beyond GSM8K. The other is creating new chaining methods that expands the serial dependency structure in our current method. We believe these two paths would offer useful extensions to the framework we introduce in this paper and further improve long-horizon reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Acknowledgments</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Shashwat Goel, Dimitris Papailiopoulos, Andrew Zhao, Harkirat Behl, Ameya Prabhu, Vibhav Vineet, Manan Tomar, Akshit Sinha, Dulhan Jayalath, Satwik Bhattamishra, Andis Draguns, Bhavya Kailkhura, Brian Bartoldson, Aleks Petrov, Ronald Clark, Fabio Pizzati, Vaibhav Balloli, and Alex Pondaven for their time and insightful discussions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
